  "notes": "Washington DC's \"R&B hippie neo-soul\" tenor on the Carvin & Ivan board. `confidence` is **low** because the research rates the entry medium-low — the production relationship and the lane position are sourced, the parameters are not. The drums are the A Touch of Jazz house grammar the entry names: \"hip-hop-gridded programmed kit with a live-feel percussion overlay — the signature of a lightly-swung kit over a programmed base; snare on 2&4, swung 16th hats, congas and shaker continuous\". \"Lightly-swung\" is why `swing.amount` sits at 0.6, below the parent's 0.63, and \"continuous\" is why the percussion density (4–7 a bar) is the highest of any programmed model in this batch. Harmony is \"4-bar cycles of the `i–iv–VII–III` family, gospel passing chords, and a modulating bridge\" — the family the entry writes out, with `susOrDimProb` for the passing chords. Melody is \"high-tenor into falsetto, moderately dense, with long melismatic tags and a spoken/whispered verse device\", so the register runs high, `endVariation` carries the tags and `emptyBarBias` carries the whispered device. Bass is \"played electric bass walking under the chord changes; light sub-reinforcement on the singles. No sustained 808\" — which is why no `bass808` block exists here: the entry rules one out in as many words. ⚠ Every drum, melody and bass number is INFERRED, `swing.amount` included. ⚠ This model shares its harmonic family with Jazmine Sullivan and Andra Day because the research gives all three the same cadence; the difference between them is authored in the drums, register and density, not invented in the chords.",
